Outbound (repo threadline) is a private, owner-only outbound sales tool hosted as a Next.js app on Vercel, with a Postgres CRM on AWS RDS. Nothing is sent automatically. The owner clicks Send on the Today screen, and a server action (gated by assertOwner) calls sendMessage in lib/engine.ts. sendMessage first claims the draft row in Postgres so a double click sends only once, then checks the stage, 24h limits, earlier replies and whether the owner is already in touch (if the in-touch lookup fails, nothing is sent). The send then goes one of three ways:
- **Email:** sent through the Gmail API, threaded with earlier messages.
- **LinkedIn:** queued as a run on a self-run Fly Chromium worker. The worker reaches LinkedIn through a Tailscale exit node on the owner's home network so LinkedIn sees a home IP. Outbound polls the run for up to 75s; if it hasn't finished, the next refresh resolves it.
- **X:** has no API. The UI opens a prefilled DM, the owner sends it by hand inside X, and Send only records it.

Separately, Vercel Cron (daily) and a Fly scheduled machine (hourly) call /api/cron with CRON_SECRET. That runs refresh(), which:
- reads Gmail for replies and bounces;
- asks the worker to read the LinkedIn inbox and recent posts;
- uses OpenRouter LLMs to score fit, write drafts and label replies;
- sources prospects from Apollo, Exa, YC and company sites;
- writes CRM updates, drafts and runtime health back to Postgres. It never sends.

On the owner's laptop, the scout CLI uses the owner's own gh, yc and bird (X) logins and POSTs what it finds to /api/import with a bearer token; that route resolves the project and persona and ingests the results. Gmail is connected through a Google OAuth callback that stores sealed tokens. LinkedIn sign-in happens inside the worker's browser, with the Settings page polling a screen-frame proxy.
